.SS Abstract
MUTEX provides a lightweight, cross\-platform mutual exclusion primitive
for synchronizing access to shared resources between multiple threads.
.PP
It is primarily intended for use alongside \f[CR]THREAD.H\f[R], allowing
threads to safely coordinate access to data and prevent race conditions.
.PP
A mutex can be created, locked, unlocked, and destroyed through a
unified API that maps to the native synchronization mechanisms of each
supported operating system.
.PP
This allows the same source code to be used across a wide range of
platforms without modification.
.PP
Typical use cases include:
.IP \(bu 2
Protecting shared variables and data structures.
.IP \(bu 2
Serializing access to files, devices, or other resources.
.IP \(bu 2
Coordinating work between threads created with THREAD.H.
.IP \(bu 2
Preventing race conditions in multithreaded applications.
.PP
MUTEX is designed to be simple, portable, and suitable for both desktop
and embedded environments.
.PP
Supported platforms:
.IP \(bu 2
FreeRTOS (define \f[CR]CMT_TRY_FREE_RTOS\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Haiku
.IP \(bu 2
OS/2 (Both 32\-bit and 16\-bit)
.IP \(bu 2
Plan9 (define \f[CR]CMT_TRY_PLAN9\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
TI\-RTOS (define \f[CR]CMT_TRY_TIRTOS\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
Unix (MacOS included)
.IP \(bu 2
VxWorks
.IP \(bu 2
Windows
.IP \(bu 2
Zephyr (define \f[CR]CMT_TRY_ZEPHYR\f[R])
.PP
If CMT indicates a \f[CR]CMT_TRY_*\f[R] macro for your platform, you
must define it manually.
.PP
The build system cannot reliably auto\-detect these platforms because
most compilers do not expose a consistent platform identification macro.
.PP
I am SO sorry about that ;\-;
.PP
Also, as an extra: If you can\(aqt find your platform in the list,
don\(aqt worry; CMT also provides a \f[CR]GO\f[R]\-like multitasking
system, or its own multithreading management, for your platform (for
example, \(dqMS\-DOS\(dq), allowing it to work on platforms that
aren\(aqt officially supported.
